1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,184
Lesson 8.7.

2
00:00:02,564 --> 00:00:04,529
Hardware-aware training.

3
00:00:05,650 --> 00:00:11,553
A model trained on a GPU at full precision
knows nothing about the chip it will

4
00:00:11,553 --> 00:00:16,784
run on: few-bit weights, noisy devices
and fixed, sparse connectivity.

5
00:00:17,164 --> 00:00:19,442
Taking it to hardware as is has a cost.

6
00:00:19,822 --> 00:00:22,997
The alternative is to train
knowing what the hardware is like.

7
00:00:23,377 --> 00:00:25,718
Today we measure three
techniques in a small

8
00:00:25,718 --> 00:00:27,953
experiment, and how much they really help.

9
00:00:29,083 --> 00:00:32,933
We use a perceptron with a hidden
layer of thirty-two neurons

10
00:00:32,933 --> 00:00:36,341
for a nonlinear classification
task in ten dimensions.

11
00:00:36,721 --> 00:00:39,915
The simulated hardware
has four-bit weights and a

12
00:00:39,915 --> 00:00:42,717
random multiplicative
error in each device.

13
00:00:43,097 --> 00:00:46,864
At full precision, the network is
correct ninety-five percent of the time.

14
00:00:47,244 --> 00:00:50,869
Taken to hardware as is,
it drops to 90.4 with ten

15
00:00:50,869 --> 00:00:54,855
percent noise, and to
eighty-eight with twenty percent.

16
00:00:55,983 --> 00:01:00,349
Quantization-aware training
computes the loss with the weights

17
00:01:00,349 --> 00:01:04,996
already quantized to four bits,
but updates a floating-point copy.

18
00:01:05,376 --> 00:01:09,691
Rounding has no useful derivative,
so the straight-through estimator is

19
00:01:09,691 --> 00:01:13,579
used: in the backward pass,
rounding is treated as the identity.

20
00:01:13,959 --> 00:01:17,755
That way, the network learns weights
that work well after rounding.

21
00:01:18,883 --> 00:01:23,357
Noise injection multiplies the
weights by a random factor at every

22
00:01:23,357 --> 00:01:27,559
training step, with the same
magnitude as the device mismatch.

23
00:01:27,939 --> 00:01:32,011
The network learns flat solutions,
which do not depend on exact values.

24
00:01:32,391 --> 00:01:37,297
In our experiment, combining
quantization and noise, the accuracy

25
00:01:37,297 --> 00:01:41,749
on hardware rises from 90.4 to
90.5 with ten percent noise,

26
00:01:42,129 --> 00:01:45,526
and from eighty-eight to
eighty-nine with twenty percent.

27
00:01:45,906 --> 00:01:49,445
Averaging three seeds, the
improvement is real but small.

28
00:01:50,550 --> 00:01:53,186
The chip has fixed, sparse connectivity,

29
00:01:53,186 --> 00:01:55,823
like the degree-sixteen graph of the Z1.

30
00:01:56,203 --> 00:02:00,793
Instead of training a dense network and
pruning it afterwards, it is better to fix

31
00:02:00,793 --> 00:02:04,992
the connection mask from the first step
and train only the allowed weights.

32
00:02:05,372 --> 00:02:09,270
With twenty-five percent of
the connections, the fixed-mask

33
00:02:09,270 --> 00:02:13,037
network is correct 92.5 percent
of the time, against 91.9

34
00:02:13,037 --> 00:02:16,142
for pruning a dense
network without retraining.

35
00:02:17,250 --> 00:02:18,236
What do we learn?

36
00:02:18,616 --> 00:02:22,971
In this experiment, the biggest
effect is the loss when moving to

37
00:02:22,971 --> 00:02:27,795
hardware, five to seven points; aware
training recovers only part of it.

38
00:02:28,175 --> 00:02:32,675
That does not contradict published
results with large models, where

39
00:02:32,675 --> 00:02:37,174
four-bit quantization-aware training
is standard, but it reminds us

40
00:02:37,174 --> 00:02:41,271
that gains depend on the model,
the task and the noise level.

41
00:02:41,651 --> 00:02:45,528
One must always measure against the
naive baseline and with several seeds.

42
00:02:46,650 --> 00:02:51,208
The recipe: model the chip, train
with a quantized forward pass

43
00:02:51,208 --> 00:02:55,548
and with device noise, fix the
sparsity mask from the start,

44
00:02:55,928 --> 00:02:59,491
as in the Z1T-style transformers
of unit ten, and report

45
00:02:59,491 --> 00:03:03,435
hardware accuracy against naive
deployment with several seeds.

46
00:03:04,550 --> 00:03:07,714
Common mistakes: evaluating
only in floating point.

47
00:03:08,094 --> 00:03:11,006
Injecting noise very different
from the real mismatch.

48
00:03:11,386 --> 00:03:14,449
Pruning after training
to fit the chip's graph.

49
00:03:14,829 --> 00:03:17,092
And claiming improvements
with a single seed.

50
00:03:18,217 --> 00:03:23,686
In summary: training with the hardware
in mind means quantizing in the forward

51
00:03:23,686 --> 00:03:28,455
pass, injecting the device noise
and fixing sparsity from the start.

52
00:03:28,835 --> 00:03:33,843
In our experiment, hardware costs five
to seven points and aware training

53
00:03:33,843 --> 00:03:38,165
recovers up to one; the fixed
mask gains a little over pruning.

54
00:03:38,545 --> 00:03:41,644
Always measure against the
baseline and with several seeds.

55
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:45,520
To practice: explain the
straight-through estimator.

56
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:49,400
Explain why noise injection
favors flat solutions.

57
00:03:49,780 --> 00:03:52,696
Explain why to fix the
mask from the start.

58
00:03:53,076 --> 00:03:56,228
And design the evaluation
of a hardware-aware method.
